{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Ripple Architecture: From World State to Sound",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "README.md",
      "pattern": "^# ripple",
      "description": "Start here for the core idea: ripple is not a catalog of authored sound effects. It runs a small simulated world, then derives sound from water, air, matter, creatures, and space."
    },
    {
      "file": "README.md",
      "pattern": "^## Architecture",
      "description": "The architecture table is the quickest module map. The runtime path is: CLI and terminal player -> preset -> World -> physics/control update -> per-sample audio readout."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/main.rs",
      "pattern": "^fn main\\(",
      "description": "The CLI selects a world and seed, then opens the live player or runs `render`, `probe` or `sync` through the same preset builder. There is one production engine; each world chooses which physical systems are present."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/main.rs",
      "pattern": "^fn arguments\\(",
      "description": "`--seed` is global, so the same seed works before or after any command. Without it, the program chooses a fresh seed. Rebuilding the same preset at the same sample rate with the same seed reproduces its initial conditions and random sequence; a restart begins that history again."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/tui.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub fn run\\(",
      "description": "The UI sends commands to the audio player and receives meters at 10 Hz. Initial construction happens before playback; later worlds build on a worker while the current world continues. Only one build runs at a time, pending requests coalesce, and generation IDs reject stale results. Retired worlds return to the UI for disposal."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/tui.rs",
      "pattern": "^    fn begin_buffer\\(",
      "description": "`Player` owns the one audible `World`. At a buffer boundary, replacement swaps worlds, resets time and fade, and applies the requested pause state together. In `next` below, pause emits silence without advancing the simulation. The tests at the end of this file compare samples to verify pause continuity and seeded restart replay."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/presets.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub const PRESETS",
      "description": "`PRESETS` is the public list of worlds. Each name is later matched in `build`, where terrain, climate, active sound sources, and critters are assembled."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/presets.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub fn build\\(",
      "description": "`build` sets terrain, climate, sources and creatures rather than authoring a final mix. It completes each preset's geological preparation before enabling live water history once at the end. Every world uses the same engine, with different initial conditions and active systems."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/world.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct World",
      "description": "`World` owns the running simulation: field water, sky/weather, turbulent voices, struck bodies, bubbles, critter choruses, reverb spaces, and a tiny control-rate scheduler."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/world.rs",
      "pattern": "^    pub fn new\\(",
      "description": "`World::new` wires the default state. Notice that most optional sound sources are `None`; presets turn them on explicitly through the assembly methods below."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/world.rs",
      "pattern": "^    pub fn enable_stream\\(",
      "description": "The `enable_*` and `add_*` methods translate preset intent into physical models. For example, wind, stream, surf, leaves, and flame are all `Turbulence` with different eddy scales."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/world.rs",
      "pattern": "^    fn step_controls\\(",
      "description": "Every 32 audio samples, the control tick advances weather and water history, routes rain impacts, creates coupled bubble packets, drives turbulent voices, moves the chime rig into contact, and updates environmental masking for the creatures."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/world.rs",
      "pattern": "^    pub fn next_sample\\(",
      "description": "`next_sample` is the audio readout. It calls `step_controls` when needed, then sums continuous fluids, ringing bodies, bubbles, living voices, and the surrounding space into a stereo sample."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/field.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct Field",
      "description": "Field owns a small virtual-pipes water grid: terrain, surface water, directional flux, springs/drains and swell. Its history state also holds retained rain, mobile sediment and the evolving bed."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/field.rs",
      "pattern": "^    pub fn step\\(",
      "description": "The water step releases retained rain, updates flux from surface-height gradients, limits outflow, and moves water and suspended sediment. Erosion/deposition changes the bed, while flow produces the next audible water events."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/field.rs",
      "pattern": "^    fn detect_events\\(",
      "description": "`detect_events` turns motion into discrete sound causes: fast water over rough rock makes bubble events, and shoaling waves create break events for surf."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/field.rs",
      "pattern": "^    pub fn geology\\(",
      "description": "`geology` runs before the audible world starts. The same water simulation erodes raw terrain by stream power, marks rough/scoured rock, drains the epoch, and leaves geography for the listener to hear."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/field.rs",
      "pattern": "^    pub fn enable_history\\(",
      "description": "History begins from the prepared terrain. `rain_on` below retains up to 80% of rain falling on open soil, bounded by reservoir capacity; the rest reaches surface water. The reservoir releases exponentially over a 40-second timescale. This is a compact retention model, and its state lasts for the running world."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/field.rs",
      "pattern": "^    fn transport_sediment\\(",
      "description": "Sediment rides the already-limited water flux: each transfer leaves one cell and enters its neighbour. `exchange_with_bed` below erodes or deposits material, updating terrain and exposed rock, which changes later flow and sound events. The tests track water and solid budgets and show that different rainfall histories remain distinct under identical later forcing."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/voices.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub const STROUHAL",
      "description": "Fluid voices share the Strouhal constant. A turbulent source stores eddy size and reference flow speed; pitch follows `f = St * v / l` rather than a hand-picked oscillator frequency."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/voices.rs",
      "pattern": "^impl Turbulence",
      "description": "`Turbulence` is a bandpassed-noise renderer for unresolved eddy cascades. Control updates drive loudness and center frequency; sample processing emits stereo air/water/flame texture."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/matter.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct Matter",
      "description": "`Matter` is deliberately tiny: wave speed, internal loss, and air loss. The audible difference between steel and wood mostly comes from loss, while body geometry supplies the modes."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/matter.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub fn bar_length_for_pitch\\(",
      "description": "`bar_length_for_pitch` is the chime-maker step. A preset may request a pitch, but that pitch becomes only a bar length; the lattice itself produces the overtone structure."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/matter.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct Body",
      "description": "`Body` is matter poured into a bar or sheet lattice. Its displacement and velocity arrays are integrated directly, so strikes, clicks, decay, stereo pickup, and overtones emerge from the model."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/matter.rs",
      "pattern": "^    fn step_sub\\(",
      "description": "`step_sub` applies the bending operator, stiffness-proportional damping, air loss, and the mounting spring. The code substeps stiff bodies to keep high-frequency modes stable."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/contacts.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct ChimeRig",
      "description": "Wind moves a suspended clapper and bars in a small-angle model. New contacts produce restitution impulses; sustained contact cannot invent repeated strikes. The mechanical ledger accounts for wind work, damping, collision loss and overlap correction. Tests check still-air silence, collision conservation, the driven energy budget and timestep sensitivity."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/world.rs",
      "pattern": "^            rig\\.step\\(",
      "description": "Here the mechanics become audible. The rig converts a fraction of collision loss into a velocity kick at a contact position; this callback applies it to the struck bar lattice. Sheltered wind keeps suspension angles small. This is a one-way coupling: lattice vibration does not push back on the suspension."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/bubbles.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct BubbleCloud",
      "description": "A cloud is eight bubbles coupled through fluid inertia, giving collective resonances. Their radii preserve the input gas volume. Mode shapes are solved at construction; playback uses exact damped modal updates without allocating. Tests recover analytic single/pair frequencies, check passive decay and verify that a collective spectral peak falls below every isolated member."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/world.rs",
      "pattern": "^        for ev in self\\.field\\.drain_events\\(\\)",
      "description": "Fast-water bubble events and breaking-wave foam excite available cloud packets; breaks also drive continuous surf. Raindrops use a separate pool of isolated plinks. Event `energy` is a normalized impulse strength, not joules, and foam volume follows a coarse entrainment rule. The bounded pools make the water-to-audio seam explicit."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/critters.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct Species",
      "description": "Species contains the authored biology of a voice: pitch range, syllable shape, phrase timing, amplitude and reverb send. The hearing scene supplies position rather than drawing a fresh stereo pan for every call."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/critters.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct Chorus",
      "description": "A chorus owns individual voices, fixed positions, propagation paths and an arrival queue. Voices retain authored species rules, but their clocks can influence one another through audible calls. The listener receives delayed, attenuated waveforms from the same geometry; clock synchrony is measured rather than guaranteed."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/critters.rs",
      "pattern": "^    pub fn process\\(",
      "description": "Receive arrivals before advancing voices. A call nudges its receiver only when its estimated level exceeds both the hearing threshold and twice the environmental masking floor. Actual syllable emission schedules later arrivals, including simultaneous callers. Tests prove that silent clock crossings send nothing, hearing follows emission plus travel time, and masking or distance can prevent a response."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/acoustics.rs",
      "pattern": "^    pub fn path\\(",
      "description": "One geometric path calculation serves both animal hearing and the listener: finite sound speed gives delay, distance gives attenuation, and an optional barrier changes transmission. The model deliberately leaves diffraction and frequency-dependent hearing unresolved."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/sky.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct Sky",
      "description": "`Sky` is slow world state: wind, gusts, rainfall, and day/night phase. It makes no sound directly; `World::step_controls` reads it to drive the audible systems."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/dsp.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub struct Space",
      "description": "Space models the diffuse reflected tail with a compact Schroeder-style reverb. Direct creature hearing and listening paths live in acoustics.rs; the world adds this inexpensive surrounding-space response to selected sources."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/audio.rs",
      "pattern": "^pub fn render_wav\\(",
      "description": "`render_wav` is the offline sink for the same `World::next_sample` path. It checks finite floating-point output before writing 16-bit stereo WAV frames, reports peak/RMS, and fails on over-range output. Use a fixed seed when comparing a change; renders use 48 kHz while live playback uses the device rate."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/world.rs",
      "pattern": "^    pub fn probe\\(",
      "description": "`probe` advances control physics without audio, exposing flow, stored water, weather and chime mechanics. `probe_sync` below advances the full sample timeline and reports the first chorus's clock order and emitted/heard/masked counts. A high clock order alone does not establish audible synchrony. Both commands accept a fixed seed."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/matter.rs",
      "pattern": "^    fn tuned_bar_rings_at_free_bar_ratios\\(",
      "description": "The tests are worth reading because they encode the project’s claim: a tuned steel bar should ring near the free-bar ratios, and material loss should produce different decay behavior without hard-coded envelopes."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/presets.rs",
      "pattern": "^    fn a_channel_is_carved_and_a_brook_runs_in_it\\(",
      "description": "This integration test checks a second emergence claim: the brook world starts from an unmarked slope, runs geology, scours rock, then produces measurable flow."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/world.rs",
      "pattern": "^    fn flowing_presets_drive_suspensions_clouds_and_active_history\\(",
      "description": "Finish with the 30-second integration audit: flowing presets must stay finite, mobilize sediment, activate churn clouds where expected, and keep wind-driven chimes inside the small-angle range. This slower test is ignored by default; run all checks with `cargo test --release -- --include-ignored`. It verifies subsystem connections alongside the focused conservation, causality and replay tests."
    }
  ]
}
